### What is the Unique Paths problem?

Count the distinct paths from the top-left to the bottom-right of an m-by-n grid, moving only right or down at each step. This is a foundational grid DP problem that teaches recurrence derivation and space optimisation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class UniquePathsDP {
    public int countPaths(int m, int n) {
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, 1);
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {
                dp[j] += dp[j - 1];
            }
        }
        return dp[n - 1];
    }
}
```

### How do you derive the recurrence?

The robot can only arrive at cell (i, j) from the cell directly above (i-1, j) or the cell directly to the left (i, j-1). Since these two sets of incoming paths are completely disjoint, the total is their sum: `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1]`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RecurrenceDerivation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int m = 3, n = 4;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) dp[i][0] = 1;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) dp[0][j] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j] + dp[i][j - 1];
            }
        System.out.println("Grid:");
        for (int[] row : dp)
            System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(row));
    }
}
```

```text
Grid:
[1, 1, 1, 1]
[1, 2, 3, 4]
[1, 3, 6, 10]
```

### How does the single-row optimisation work?

Each row depends only on the previous row and the cell to the left. A single array of size n suffices. Before updating, `dp[j]` holds the above-cell value from the previous row; `dp[j-1]` was already updated in this row and holds the left-cell value. Adding them updates `dp[j]` in place.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SingleRowTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int m = 3, n = 7;
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, 1);
        System.out.println("Row 0: " + java.util.Arrays.toString(dp));
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) dp[j] += dp[j - 1];
            System.out.println("Row " + i + ": "
                + java.util.Arrays.toString(dp));
        }
        System.out.println("Answer: " + dp[n - 1]); // 28
    }
}
```

```text
Row 0: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
Row 1: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
Row 2: [1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28]
Answer: 28
```

### What is the combinatorial closed-form solution?

Every path is a sequence of exactly (m-1) down moves and (n-1) right moves, for a total of (m+n-2) moves. Choosing which moves are down gives C(m+n-2, m-1). This formula computes the answer in O(min(m, n)) time without building a grid.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CombinatorialPaths {
    public long countPaths(int m, int n) {
        long result = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            result = result * (n - 1 + i) / i;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CombinatorialPaths cp = new CombinatorialPaths();
        System.out.println("3x7: " + cp.countPaths(3, 7));     // 28
        System.out.println("10x10: " + cp.countPaths(10, 10));  // 48620
    }
}
```

```text
3x7: 28
10x10: 48620
```

### How is this related to Pascal's Triangle?

The filled DP grid is a rotated Pascal's Triangle. Each cell dp[i][j] equals the binomial coefficient C(i+j, i). This connection provides mathematical insight: the grid of path counts has exactly the same structure as binomial coefficients.

```java
// Pascal's Triangle connection:
//
// dp[0][0]=C(0,0)=1   dp[0][1]=C(1,0)=1   dp[0][2]=C(2,0)=1
// dp[1][0]=C(1,1)=1   dp[1][1]=C(2,1)=2   dp[1][2]=C(3,1)=3
// dp[2][0]=C(2,2)=1   dp[2][1]=C(3,2)=3   dp[2][2]=C(4,2)=6
//
// Each cell: C(i+j, i) = C(i+j, j)
```

### What are the edge cases?

A 1x1 grid has one path (start equals end). A 1xN or Mx1 grid has exactly one path (all right or all down). Very large grids may overflow int in the combinatorial formula.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PathEdgeCases2 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UniquePathsDP solver = new UniquePathsDP();
        System.out.println("1x1: " + solver.countPaths(1, 1));   // 1
        System.out.println("1x10: " + solver.countPaths(1, 10)); // 1
        System.out.println("2x2: " + solver.countPaths(2, 2));   // 2
        System.out.println("4x4: " + solver.countPaths(4, 4));   // 20
    }
}
```

```text
1x1: 1
1x10: 1
2x2: 2
4x4: 20
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is initialising the first row or column to 0 instead of 1, which zeros out the entire grid. Another mistake is updating the rolling row right-to-left instead of left-to-right, which uses the wrong left-neighbor value.

```java
// WRONG: first row initialized to 0
int[] dp = new int[n]; // all 0 by default
// dp[j] += dp[j-1] starts from 0, everything stays 0!

// CORRECT: fill with 1
int[] dp = new int[n];
java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, 1);
```

### How would you apply this in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a loan approval workflow moves through stages (rows) and departments (columns). Risk analysts count the distinct approval routes to understand the process complexity.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WorkflowRouteAnalyzer {
    public int countRoutes(int stages, int departments) {
        int[] dp = new int[departments];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, 1);
        for (int i = 1; i < stages; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j < departments; j++)
                dp[j] += dp[j - 1];
        return dp[departments - 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WorkflowRouteAnalyzer analyzer = new WorkflowRouteAnalyzer();
        System.out.println("5 stages, 4 depts: "
            + analyzer.countRoutes(5, 4)); // 35
    }
}
```

```text
5 stages, 4 depts: 35
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know all three approaches and their trade-offs. The full grid DP is simplest. The single-row saves memory to O(n). The combinatorial formula is fastest at O(min(m,n)) but risks overflow and does not extend to obstacle variants. They should also recognise the Pascal's Triangle connection.

```java
// Approach comparison:
// 1. Full grid DP:   O(m*n) time, O(m*n) space   - simplest
// 2. Single-row DP:  O(m*n) time, O(n) space      - practical
// 3. Combinatorial:  O(min(m,n)) time, O(1) space  - fastest
//    (but risks overflow, no obstacle support)
```
